      "metadata": {
        "name": "ARGELATI POOLS -  Between Water and Sky",
        "author": "Maxence Werp, Enea Manghi, Michele Beltrami, Matilde Invernizzi",
        "year": "Spring 2026",
        "tags": [
          "architecture",
          "model"
        ],
        "description": "At the edge of the Navigli, an old pool opens itself to the city again. A cable holds the sky. A membrane learns when to close.<br><br>The project transforms the Argelati swimming pool into a year-round public threshold, neither fully inside nor fully outside, but always inhabited. A single tensioned strand, 42 metres long, carries a retractable PTFE roof that folds back on warm days and closes against winter. Water is present in all seasons. The building breathes with the city.<br><br>Structure here is not hidden, it is the architecture. Steel nodes, cables, and cushions make the logic of enclosure visible. Everything is prefabricated, reversible, designed to be undone as precisely as it was assembled.<br><br>What was seasonal becomes civic. What was closed becomes an invitation.<br><br>Instagram Handles:<br>Maxence Werp @maxencewerp<br>Enea Manghi @enea.mng<br>Michele Beltrami @beltramicheli<br>Matilde Invernizzi<br>",
        "course": "ACHITECTURE DESIGN AND CONSTRUCTION STUDIO",
        "track": "Laurea Magistrale, Y1",
        "prof": "Zanelli Novati"
      }
    },
